Short:    HD-Installer for Addams Family
Author:   Codetapper/Action (codetapper@hotmail.com),
          DJ Mike (djmike@crystalmike.com)
Version:  2.0
Type:     game/patch

This patch applies to "Addams Family" © 1992 Ocean - 1 disk.
Thanks to Carlo Pirri and Chris Vella for sending the originals!

Now includes "The Backgrounds Edition" - an enhanced version by DJ Mike that
restores the backgrounds that were omitted from the original release.

When installing, you will be asked if you want to install the standard
version, or The Backgrounds Edition. The latter can be installed using the
original game disk - the extra files needed are contained in the install.

If you are upgrading from v1.0-1.3 you will need to reinstall.

REQUIREMENTS:
- Slave requires WHDLoad V20+.
- Game requires 512k chip mem (+0.9Mb other memory for preload option)
- The Backgrounds Edition requires 768k chip mem for OCS or 2MB for AGA

FEATURES:
- Full load from HD with real files
- Backgrounds restored - install "The Backgrounds Edition" to use.
  Select mode from splash screen or with CUSTOM2.
  AGA Parallax requires A1200/4000, and 2MB chip memory config selected
  OCS Scroll With Level requires at least 768k chip memory config selected
- Rob Northen Copylock removed
- Access fault fixed
- Self-modifying code removed (caused Gomez sprite glitches)
- Can run with caches for better speed
- Empty/CPU-dependent loops removed
- Removed anti-Action Replay dodgy copperlist entries
- Music player DMA delay fixed
- Draw code optimisations added
- Missing blitter waits added
- Decruncher and stack moved to fast memory if available
- Instructions included
- Trainers: Infinite lives, hearts and in-game keys:
  Help to toggle infinite lives, Del to toggle energy,
  F6 to give Fez, F7 to give speed boots.
  The screen will flash green when a cheat is enabled and red if disabled.
- 2 different RomIcons, NewIcons and GlowIcons (all created by Frank!)
- Quit option (default key is 'F10')
- Source available: https://github.com/DJ-Mike-101/WHDL-Releases

THE BACKGROUNDS EDITION

Ever since its release in 1992, the Amiga version of this game has lacked
the backgrounds of its Atari ST, SNES and Megadrive brethren. The reasons
for this are muddied by time. I gather from talking to James Higgins, the
original coder, that dropping the backgrounds was very much due to time
constraint. They had wanted to use the Amiga playfield and sprite hardware
to scroll the game at 50fps, despite the rest of the game only redrawing at
25fps or less. They also wanted Gomez to be able to move in front and behind
scenery. While this is all possible to achieve, the backgrounds became a
problem. The parallax scroll lagged the rest of the level - it has to happen
in the redraw code, not hardware, due to the game using a single 32 colour
playfield. Gomez's sprite can also only be behind the entire level or in
front of it. With backgrounds, Gomez would disappear entirely! 

Dropping the backgrounds was realistically the fastest path to release even
if it was not the outcome they wanted - anything else would mean sacrificing
frame rate, or taking more time to write a better solution. Ocean chose to
just ship it.

After some investigation, and based on initial finds on an old EAB forum
thread, I discovered that the code for rendering the backgrounds has been
buried in the Amiga version all this time - and simply disabled in game.
In fact, the backgrounds are still used ever so slightly, to draw the title
options and a couple of other interstitial screens. I had to jig some memory
about, because they share the same space as the music, but after adjusting
pointers and reactivating some routines, I was pleasantly surprised to find
the backgrounds sprung to life, with a half-scrolling parallax effect. But,
it was immediately apparent this pre-dated the game switching to hardware
scrolling, because they sure looked janky!

Due to the game using 32 colours on OCS Amigas, it is not possible to have
the backgrounds scroll independently in their own playfield. Therefore I
have compromised by having the backgrounds scroll at the same speed as the
level.

But on AGA Amigas, there are 3 more bitplanes available, which is enough for
the (mostly 8 colour) backgrounds to scroll at half speed as intended. So if
you play on an A1200/4000 you will get the full parallax experience!

I have reworked a lot of the code for AGA so the graphics are drawn using
FMODE 1/32-bit mode for improved performance. Making the parallax scrolling
work while also retaining the 32 colour foreground requires having 32 copies
of the background in memory at any time, meaning a lot of careful memory
management. The game barely fits in the 2MB chip RAM, but it does fit!

Gomez disappearing behind the entire level is still a problem, so that too
has had a compromise - instead of flipping sprite priority, a stipple is
applied to the sprite to give the illusion of going behind the scenery.

Various other bugs have been fixed including self-modifying code and missing
blitter waits, so this really is, hopefully, much closer to what the Amiga
should have gotten for this classic game.

This is not the end of the road for this project - I have some ideas for
other improvements I'm going to explore. As they say, watch this space!

